---
import BaseLayout from "theme/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const lang = "en";
const dir = "ltr";
const slug = "easter-2027-date";
const seoTitle = "Easter 2027 Date | When Is Easter Sunday in 2027?";
const pageDescription = "Easter Sunday 2027 is on 28 March. See Good Friday, Easter Monday, and how the Easter date is calculated each year.";

const pubDate = "2026-10-25T15:50:00+05:00";
const updatedDate = "2026-10-25T16:00:00+05:00";

const meta = {
  seoTitle,
  exactTitle: true,
  description: pageDescription,
  keywords: "easter 2027 date, when is easter 2027, easter sunday 2027, good friday 2027, easter monday 2027, orthodox easter 2027 date",
  pubDate,
  updatedDate,
  author: "Time Intelligence Team",
  faqs: [
    {
      q: "When is Easter Sunday 2027?",
      a: "Sunday 28 March 2027."
    },
    {
      q: "When is Good Friday 2027?",
      a: "Friday 26 March 2027."
    },
    {
      q: "Why does Easter move every year?",
      a: "Because Western Easter is set by the first Sunday after the first full moon on or after 21 March, so it falls on a different date each year."
    },
    {
      q: "Is Orthodox Easter on the same day in 2027?",
      a: "No. Orthodox Easter in 2027 is Sunday 2 May 2027."
    }
  ]
};

// Exactly 4 rows for the Holy Week Easter table
const EASTER_DAYS_2027 = [
  { day: "Good Friday", date: "Friday, 26 March 2027" },
  { day: "Holy Saturday", date: "Saturday, 27 March 2027" },
  { day: "Easter Sunday", date: "Sunday, 28 March 2027" },
  { day: "Easter Monday", date: "Monday, 29 March 2027" }
];

const easterEventSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Event",
  "name": "Easter Sunday 2027",
  "startDate": "2027-03-28"
};
---

<BaseLayout
  lang={lang}
  dir={dir}
  title={seoTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  slug={slug}
  meta={meta}
  enableArticleSchema={true}
  enableFaqSchema={true}
  allowAds={true}
>
  <!-- Schema: Dedicated Event for Easter Sunday 2027 Only -->
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(easterEventSchema)} />

  <!-- Hero Banner -->
  <div class="hero-banner">
    <h1 class="hero-banner-title">Easter 2027 Date</h1>
    <p style="color: #bdd2dc; font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.2vw, 1.25rem); max-width: 680px; margin: 12px auto 0;">
      Western Easter dates, Good Friday, Easter Monday, ecclesiastical calendar computus, and Orthodox Pascha comparisons.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="max-width: 860px; margin: 0 auto; padding-top: 24px;">

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         QUICK ANSWER (SERVER-RENDERED DIRECTLY BENEATH H1)
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <section class="quick-answer-card" style="background: var(--panel); border: 2px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 14px; padding: 22px 26px; box-shadow: var(--shadow); margin-bottom: 32px;">
      <h2 style="margin: 0 0 10px; font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--accent); font-family: 'Space Grotesk', system-ui, sans-serif;">
        Quick Answer
      </h2>
      <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.65; color: #ffffff; font-weight: 500;">
        Easter Sunday 2027 is on Sunday, 28 March 2027. Good Friday is Friday, 26 March 2027, and Easter Monday is Monday, 29 March 2027 in the UK and other countries that observe it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Hero Image Banner -->
    <div style="margin: 0 0 36px; text-align: center;">
      <img
        src="/assets/easter-2027-date-hero.webp"
        alt="Spring calendar marking Easter Sunday on 28 March 2027"
        width="960"
        height="540"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--line); box-shadow: var(--shadow);"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         MAIN CONTENT (STRICT ORDER)
         1. H2: When is Easter in 2027?
         2. H2: How is the Easter date calculated?
         3. H2: Western Easter vs Orthodox Easter
         4. H2: What is closed at Easter?
         5. H2: Frequently Asked Questions About Easter 2027
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <article class="article-content">

      <!-- Section 1: When is Easter in 2027? -->
      <h2>When is Easter in 2027?</h2>
      <p>
        In 2027, Western Christian churches celebrate Easter Sunday on <strong>Sunday, 28 March 2027</strong>. The broader Easter weekend and Holy Week observances span the following key dates:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrapper" style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 24px 0 16px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
        <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.95rem;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background: #082030; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">
              <th scope="col" style="padding: 14px 16px; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 800; font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;">Day</th>
              <th scope="col" style="padding: 14px 16px; color: #38bdf8; font-weight: 800; font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; white-space: nowrap;">Date</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {EASTER_DAYS_2027.map((item, idx) => (
              <tr style={`border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06); ${idx % 2 === 1 ? 'background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);' : ''}`}>
                <td style="padding: 14px 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #ffffff;">{item.day}</td>
                <td style="padding: 14px 16px; white-space: nowrap; font-family: 'Space Grotesk', monospace; color: item.day === 'Easter Sunday' ? '#00d4aa' : '#bdd2dc'; font-weight: item.day === 'Easter Sunday' ? '800' : '500';">
                  {item.date}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <!-- Mandatory Clarification Immediately After Table -->
      <p style="margin: 16px 0 32px; font-style: italic; color: #bdd2dc; line-height: 1.6; padding: 12px 16px; background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.05); border-left: 3px solid #38bdf8; border-radius: 4px;">
        Easter Monday is not a US federal holiday.
      </p>

      <!-- Holy Week Diagram -->
      <figure style="margin: 32px 0 36px; text-align: center;">
        <img
          src="/assets/easter-2027-holy-week.webp"
          alt="Timeline from Good Friday to Easter Monday in 2027"
          width="800"
          height="450"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--line); box-shadow: var(--shadow);"
        />
        <figcaption style="font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 10px; font-style: italic;">
          Good Friday 26 March, Easter Sunday 28 March, Easter Monday 29 March 2027.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <!-- Section 2: How is the Easter date calculated? -->
      <h2>How is the Easter date calculated?</h2>
      <p>
        Unlike holidays anchored to a fixed Gregorian calendar day (such as Christmas on 25 December), Western Easter is a movable feast determined by an ecclesiastical algorithm established at the First Council of Nicaea in 325 AD:
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left: 24px; margin-bottom: 20px; line-height: 1.7;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>The Ecclesiastical Spring Equinox:</strong> In Western Christian computus, the vernal equinox is permanently fixed to <strong>21 March</strong>, regardless of the precise minute when astronomical equinox occurs.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>The Paschal Full Moon:</strong> Western Easter is celebrated on the first Sunday immediately following the first ecclesiastical full moon that occurs on or after 21 March.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>Ecclesiastical vs Astronomical Full Moon:</strong> The ecclesiastical full moon is calculated using historical 19-year lunar cycles (the Metonic cycle) and church calendar tables. It is a defined calendar rule and is not necessarily identical to the exact moment of the observed astronomical full moon in the sky.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>Date Range:</strong> Because of this mathematical framework, Western Easter Sunday always falls between 22 March and 25 April. In 2027, the ecclesiastical full moon occurs on Monday 22 March, making the following Sunday—28 March 2027—Easter Sunday.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Section 3: Western Easter vs Orthodox Easter -->
      <h2>Western Easter vs Orthodox Easter</h2>
      <p>
        Western Christian churches (including Roman Catholic, Protestant, and Anglican traditions) and Eastern Orthodox churches follow distinct calendar calculations, frequently resulting in different dates for Easter:
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left: 24px; margin-bottom: 20px; line-height: 1.7;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>Western Easter Sunday:</strong> Sunday, 28 March 2027.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>Orthodox Easter Sunday (Pascha):</strong> Sunday, 2 May 2027.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These are distinct observance dates that must not be merged into a single generic Easter date for 2027. The difference occurs because Eastern Orthodox churches calculate the Paschal full moon based on the older Julian calendar rather than the Gregorian calendar, and adhere strictly to the historical decree that Pascha must follow the Jewish celebration of Passover. In 2027, this creates a five-week difference between Western Easter and Orthodox Pascha.
      </p>

      <!-- Section 4: What is closed at Easter? -->
      <h2>What is closed at Easter?</h2>
      <p>
        Statutory holiday recognition and business operating schedules during Easter vary considerably across international jurisdictions:
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left: 24px; margin-bottom: 20px; line-height: 1.7;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>United Kingdom &amp; Commonwealth Countries:</strong> Good Friday (26 March) and Easter Monday (29 March) are statutory public and bank holidays across England, Wales, and Northern Ireland (with Good Friday designated in Scotland). Government offices, banks, and many private businesses close or operate on reduced Sunday schedules.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>United States:</strong> Neither Good Friday nor Easter Monday is a US federal holiday under Title 5 of the United States Code. Federal civil service offices, USPS mail delivery, and national financial markets generally maintain normal operations on Easter Monday, although US stock exchanges (NYSE and Nasdaq) close on Good Friday. Approximately a dozen individual US states recognize Good Friday as a state holiday.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">
          <strong>Schools &amp; Retail:</strong> Many public school districts and universities schedule their annual spring break around late March or early April. Retailers, grocery stores, and transport operators set their own operating policies, with some choosing to close specifically on Easter Sunday.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because public-holiday status and operational hours vary by region, employer, and service, readers should always verify localized calendars before planning travel or expecting closures.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/ramadan-2027-dates/">Ramadan</a> is also expected to begin in February 2027, with Eid al-Fitr expected in March, subject to moon sighting. These dates and Easter are separate observances; any overlap is a calendar coincidence only. If you are coordinating global schedules, tracking holidays, or planning cross-border operations across spring 2027, you can consult our complete <a href="/holidays-2027/">Holidays in 2027</a> hub, calculate working intervals with our <a href="/days-between-dates-calculator/">Days Between Dates Calculator</a>, or coordinate meeting hours across time zones using the <a href="/">GMT to EST Converter</a>.
      </p>

      <!-- Section 5: FAQs -->
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Easter 2027</h2>
      <div class="faq-container" style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin-top: 20px;">
        {meta.faqs.map((faq) => (
          <details style="background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.05rem; cursor: pointer; color: var(--text);">
              {faq.q}
            </summary>
            <p style="margin: 12px 0 0; color: #bdd2dc; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6;">
              {faq.a}
            </p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

    </article>

  </div>

  <style>
    .article-content {
      line-height: 1.75;
      color: #bdd2dc;
    }
    .article-content h2 {
      color: #ffffff;
      font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem);
      margin: 36px 0 16px;
      padding-bottom: 8px;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
      font-family: "Space Grotesk", system-ui, sans-serif;
    }
    .article-content p {
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }
    .article-content a {
      color: var(--accent);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }
    .article-content a:hover {
      color: #ffffff;
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
